    move eq eq9 scale 1.5 duration 0.01

    move nm1 nm2 scale 1.7 duration 0.01

    move k1 k2 scale 1.6 duration 0.01

    move pform scale 1.3 duration 0.01

    move ell scale 1.4 duration 0.01

    card title "Fermat's Last Theorem"

A: Two squares can add up to a square. So why can two cubes never add up to a cube?
    draw tri

B: Like the three, four, five triangle?
    draw m3 m4 m5 parallel true

A: Exactly. Three squared plus four squared is five squared: nine plus sixteen.
    draw eq eq9

B: Are there many triples like that?
    draw triples_box triples_line_1 triples_line_2 triples_line_3

A: Infinitely many. Pick any two whole numbers, and an old recipe hands you a new triple.
    draw triples_line_4

    erase triples m3 m4 m5 tri eq9

    move eq by [-260,-120] scale 1.6 duration 1

A: Now raise the power to three, and the triangle is no help any more.
    morph eq tex "a^3 + b^3 = c^3" duration 2

B: Surely, if I search long enough, I'll find two cubes that work.
    highlight eq

    fade eq to 0 duration 0.5

    move eq by [322,70] duration 0.3

    ask "Six cubed plus eight cubed: how much is that?" estimate.min 500 estimate.max 900 estimate.value 728 store g
        right: Seven hundred and twenty-eight. Nine cubed is seven hundred and twenty-nine: missed by one.
        wrong: You said {g}. It is seven hundred and twenty-eight, and nine cubed is seven twenty-nine: missed by one.

    draw nm1 nm2

A: So close, and still no cube. In 1637 Pierre de Fermat claimed no power above two ever works.
    draw fermat

    erase fermat nm1 nm2

    fade eq to 1 duration 0.5

    morph eq tex "a^n + b^n = c^n" duration 1.5

A: He wrote it in the margin of an old Greek maths book, the Arithmetica of Diophantus.
    draw page printed pform mline

B: And beside it: he had a truly marvellous proof, but the margin was too narrow?
    draw note

A: Yes. Nobody ever found that proof, and the note cost mathematicians three hundred and fifty-eight years.
    highlight note_end

    erase page printed pform mline note eq

A: Here are the powers left to beat. Fermat himself knocked out four, with a trick called infinite descent.
    draw powers

A: Suppose a solution exists. From it he built a smaller one, and from that a smaller one again, forever.
    draw descent_box descent_line_1 descent_line_2 descent_line_3 descent_line_4

B: But whole numbers can't shrink forever. You hit one, and stop.
    highlight descent_line_4

    erase descent

A: Right, so there was never a first solution. And  (@fade n4 n8 n12 n16 n20 to 0.2@)four takes eight, twelve, sixteen and twenty down with it.

A: Euler did three, in  (@fade n3 n6 n9 n15 n18 n21 to 0.2@)1770, and that clears every multiple of three.

    draw a10

    fade n10 to 0.2 duration 0.6

    fade powers a10 to 0 duration 0.8

    draw still

    ask "A tenth power is a fifth power of a square, so ten falls with five. Which others still need a proof? Tap them." on still stage own store k
        right: Five, seven and thirteen. Fourteen falls with seven, twenty-two with eleven, fifteen with three.
        wrong: {k} of {k.total} on the first try. Fourteen falls with seven, twenty-two with eleven.

    erase still

    fade n3 n4 n6 n8 n9 n10 n12 n14 n15 n16 n18 n20 n21 n22 a10 to 0.2 duration 0.6

    fade n5 n7 n11 n13 n17 n19 to 1 duration 0.6

B: So only the odd primes are left.
    draw a14 a22 parallel true

    erase a10 a14 a22

A: Sophie Germain, around 1820, showed that for many primes, a solution would need that prime to divide a, b or c.
    draw germain

    erase germain

A: Then Kummer hit a wall. The proofs worked in bigger number systems, where numbers can split into primes in more than one way.
    highlight n5 n7 n11 n13 n17 n19

    fade powers to 0.1 duration 0.6

A: Six is two times three. But in one such system, it is also this.
    draw k1 k2

B: Two different ways to split the same number? That breaks everything.
    highlight k2

    erase k1 k2

    fade n3 n4 n6 n8 n9 n10 n12 n14 n15 n16 n18 n20 n21 n22 to 0.2 duration 0.5

    fade n5 n7 n11 n13 n17 n19 to 1 duration 0.5

    draw open open_label

A: Kummer's repair covered every prime below a hundred except thirty-seven, fifty-nine and sixty-seven. Infinitely many still stood.
    blocking false

    fade n5 n7 n11 n13 n17 n19 to 0.2 duration 1.2

    pause 2.2

    highlight open effect pulse duration 2.5

    clear

A: The way through came from somewhere else entirely: from curves like this one.
    draw ax curve

B: y squared equals x cubed minus x. That's an elliptic curve?
    draw ell

A: It is. Now forget the drawing. Count its whole-number solutions modulo a prime, in clock arithmetic.
    draw tab_top

A: Those counts make a fingerprint. Modular means some hugely symmetric function carries the very same fingerprint.
    draw tab_fp

    erase tab_top tab_fp

B: And in the 1950s Taniyama and Shimura guessed that every curve like this is modular?
    highlight curve

A: Right. Then in 1984 Gerhard Frey had a wild idea: t (@morph ell tex "y^2 = x(x-a^p)(x+b^p)" duration 2.5@)ake a supposed solution, and build a curve from it.

B: Its curve crosses the axis at a to the p, and minus b to the p?
    draw roots lr1 lr2

A: Yes, and with those huge numbers built in, it's so strange that Ken Ribet proved, in 1986, it cannot be modular.
    highlight curve effect pulse

    clear

B: So: a solution gives Frey's curve, and Ribet says Frey's curve is not modular.
    draw c_sol a_sf c_frey a_fn c_not l_ribet

A: But if every curve like that is modular…
    draw a_fm c_mod

B: …then Frey's curve is both at once, which is impossible.
    draw clash xmark

A: And Andrew Wiles proved enough of that old guess to cover Frey's curve.
    draw wiles

    erase wiles

A: He announced it in Cambridge in 1993. A gap turned up; with Richard Taylor he closed it, and it was published in 1995.
    draw l_wiles

B: So nobody ever checked all the numbers?
    point at.ref c_sol gesture circle

A: Nobody had to. Any solution would build a c (@draw nosol@)urve that must be modular and cannot be. So there is no solution.

    quiz "Suppose Ribet's step had failed, and Frey's curve could be modular. Would Wiles's theorem alone prove Fermat?"
        right: No. Wiles shows the curve is modular; only Ribet's 'it cannot be' turns that into a contradiction.
        wrong: A proof by contradiction needs two claims that cannot both be true.
        * Yes: the curve is modular
        + No: there is no clash left
        * Yes: descent covers the rest

